WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.734 --> 00:00:02.734
-You guys, we're back here
with "Late Night."

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:04.033
We're chatting with Bowen Yang.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:06.000
It's very exciting. I know it
must be also exhausting.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.634
But when you're off from "SNL,"
you were going over to London

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:10.968
because you were working on
"Wicked."

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:13.467
-Yes! Oh, my gosh.
It's so cool.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:15.734
-This is amazing. This is
a film version of "Wicked."

00:00:15.734 --> 00:00:17.167
-Yes.
-You're not just going over

00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:19.601
to do, like, a show over there.
-Which I would happily do.

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:22.601
Especially because the cast
on the West End of "Wicked"

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:25.300
just fully digs into
the British accent

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:26.467
and it's so funny.

00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:27.567
They're like,
[ In British accent ]

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:28.701
"We're going to see
the Wizard of Oz."

00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:30.400
[ Laughter ]

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.167
It's really funny.

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:33.400
But it's great.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.033
No, we're doing the movie
and it's crazy.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:38.267
-And this is a show that
meant something to you.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:39.334
-Yes, okay.

00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.100
So the second time I ever
came to New York,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.901
the first time I went
into Manhattan,

00:00:43.901 --> 00:00:45.167
it was 2005.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:47.234
"Wicked" had just come out
a year and a half before.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:49.067
I was obsessed with it.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.868
I was besotted by the songs.

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:54.634
And then I came to New York

00:00:54.634 --> 00:00:58.467
and then I begged my parents
to buy tickets.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:00:59.534
It was the hottest ticket
in town.

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.567
We couldn't go.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.601
And I was like, "Well,
can we at least drive up

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:07.834
to the Gershwin Theater
so I can press my face

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:09.467
up against the glass?"

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:11.634
And so we went.

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:13.901
It was the most pathetic sight.

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.701
-'Cause it's not like, I mean,

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.234
from my knowledge of
Broadway theaters,

00:01:17.234 --> 00:01:19.834
the glass, you don't
get to see the set or anything.

00:01:19.834 --> 00:01:23.100
-You don't get to see the set,
but you get to see the lobby.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.434
You get to sort of touch things
that touch the floor

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:29.734
that lead to the stage,
so you're kind of like,

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:32.534
you're sort of like
a couple degrees away.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:34.801
And so we couldn't do that,
but what we did do was,

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:37.300
we came to this building for
the NBC studio tour.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.601
We got to go over to
the "SNL" studio

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:41.400
and sit there, and I was like,

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.901
"This isn't as cool as
'Wicked.'"

00:01:42.901 --> 00:01:44.634
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:45.901
-It must be very nice --

00:01:45.901 --> 00:01:47.701
Do you ever reflect back on
the fact that you're

00:01:47.701 --> 00:01:50.033
now working on the two things
that you --

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:51.968
one, were on
an underwhelming tour of

00:01:51.968 --> 00:01:53.267
and two, you couldn't get
tickets for?

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:55.400
-Yes, I mean, it's crazy.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.834
It's a crazy full-circle.

00:01:56.834 --> 00:02:01.901
It's also amazing that they're
both Universal Studios projects.

00:02:01.901 --> 00:02:04.300
Watch Peacock.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.501
[ Laughter ]

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:06.767
[ Laughs ] But...

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.267
-So much synergy!
-So much synergy!

00:02:08.267 --> 00:02:10.534
-The synergy is red-hot
coming off you.

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:12.067
-I promise it's genuine.

00:02:12.067 --> 00:02:15.767
Like, I love Peacock so much.
I really do.

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:17.767
"Poker Face"
starring Natasha Lyonne

00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.367
is premiering on it.
-Really good.

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:20.834
-It's really good.

00:02:20.834 --> 00:02:21.968
But no, it's crazy.

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:23.300
It is full circle, though.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.968
-Obviously, this is a musical.

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:28.868
Is there choreography involved
with your role?

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:30.133
There's choreography involved.

00:02:30.133 --> 00:02:32.567
So Jon M. Chu
is directing the film,

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:34.334
and he is still working
with choreographers

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:37.701
that he's been with
since "Step Up 2."

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:40.200
And I was really intimidated
going into this.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.300
I was like, "I'm not a dancer.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.300
I don't know really
how to move."

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.367
-I would say,
just from watching "SNL,"

00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:46.601
I do feel like you are

00:02:46.601 --> 00:02:49.167
very good at sketch comedy
choreography, right?

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:50.467
-It's just the arms.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:52.901
They're never panning down.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.067
-Oh, that's interesting.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.033
I have been fooled
by your arm work then.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:57.133
-Exactly.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:00.133
That's all sketch comedy dancing
really is.

00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:03.100
But I was really scared to do
this, and then I went in

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.267
and I thought I was doing okay,

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:09.200
and then these
lovely choreographers

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.934
are validating you
at every turn.

00:03:10.934 --> 00:03:14.200
They're going, "That's amazing!
Oh, my goodness!"

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.734
And then Christopher Scott,
the choreographer,

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:19.367
looked me in the eyes and said,
"Bowen, you're a dancer."

00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:21.367
And I was like,
"Wow, I felt that."

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.501
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:27.000
The next week, we're all on set
and some of the other actors

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.234
who are also not trained,
professional dancers and I

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:31.300
are talking about
the choreography.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.834
We're like, "Oh,
it's pretty complicated,

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:34.300
but I think I have
a good handle on it."

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.868
And then two of them were like,
"Yeah, and Christopher Scott

00:03:37.868 --> 00:03:40.667
looked me in the eyes and
was like, 'You're a dancer.'"

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:42.234
I was like...

00:03:42.234 --> 00:03:45.467
oh, so I'm not that special,
I guess?

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:47.534
But it's gonna be great.

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:48.767
It's gonna be so good.

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:50.300
-You're not selling this movie!

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:53.667
"I just cannot stress to you

00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:55.667
how much the choreographer
lies to us."

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:57.000
-Yes!

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.667
Lies, but also ultimately
gives you the boost you need.

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.100
-I think the boost
is more important.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.968
In the early days of
learning choreography,

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:05.501
I think a boost is far better.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.968
I would respond better to a
boost than the hard truth.

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:09.300
-Totally.
-Yeah.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.934
-And watch me get cut out of
the movie from dancing.

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.701
-Well, the best is,
every shot of you dancing

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:15.100
is definitely gonna be
from here up.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.133
-Yeah.
-A lot of arm work.

00:04:16.133 --> 00:04:17.400
-And then people at home
will be like,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.701
"That's amazing sketch dancing
in this movie."

00:04:19.701 --> 00:04:20.901
-And then it's gonna
cut to, like,

00:04:20.901 --> 00:04:23.100
definitely someone else's legs
moving real fast.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.767
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.100
-You get to go to London.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.234
I know it must be hard because

00:04:27.234 --> 00:04:28.634
you're obviously working
when you're there.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:29.834
Are you enjoying
the city at all?

00:04:29.834 --> 00:04:31.567
-I am. I'm really
enjoying the city.

00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:35.000
The only note I have for London
is that, um,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.567
they don't understand what
the concept of iced coffee is.

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:40.934
-Is that true?
Even in this day and age?

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.334
-I kind of think so.

00:04:42.334 --> 00:04:44.267
Anywhere in Europe, you go,
"Can I have an iced coffee?"

00:04:44.267 --> 00:04:45.634
and they look at you like

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.334
you've asked for, like, a
lobster thermidor or something.

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:50.367
I'm like, "I just want
coffee on ice."

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:52.300
And they go, "So an Americano?"

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:55.968
I'm like, "No, just give me
an iced coffee, please."

00:04:55.968 --> 00:04:59.901
And they also -- London is
trying to like,

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:01.534
veer in the other direction.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:03.601
They should embrace tradition,
reject modernity,

00:05:03.601 --> 00:05:04.901
go back to meat pies.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:07.033
You can't find a good
meat pie in London.

00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:10.067
-Are you looking?
-I'm looking for a meat pie.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:13.834
And it's not even
a Sweeney Todd thing anymore.

00:05:13.834 --> 00:05:15.501
There's no, like,
worst pies in London.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:16.767
There's no pies in London.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:19.868
-Where does this craving
for a meat pie come from?

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:22.100
Is it just an expectation
you had of London?

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.334
-It's that.

00:05:23.334 --> 00:05:26.133
It's me being like,
a Commonwealth baby,

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:27.501
being born in Australia,

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:28.534
going to Canada.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:31.601
Meat pies are a thing that,
like,

00:05:31.601 --> 00:05:34.334
I've accepted is never
gonna happen in America.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.334
Americans just don't care
for meat pies.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:40.901
But people who have
the Queen's face on their money,

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:42.434
like, love it, you know?

00:05:42.434 --> 00:05:43.868
-So bring back the meat pies!

00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:44.834
-Bring back the meat pies.

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:46.133
-Alright, well that is --

00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:47.534
So if there's anything to
take away from this,

00:05:47.534 --> 00:05:49.501
it's watch Peacock,
bring back the meat pies.

00:05:49.501 --> 00:05:50.634
-Yep.

00:05:50.634 --> 00:05:52.534
Bring back the meat pies
and Lenny Hochstein

00:05:52.534 --> 00:05:54.267
is dead in this town.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:55.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.567 --> 00:05:56.701
-Thank you so much.

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:58.000
It's always such a pleasure
to see you.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.467
You guys, that's Bowen Yang.

00:05:59.467 --> 00:06:02.234
"SNL" this Saturday.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.968
Stream live on Peacock!

00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:06.534
With host Michael B. Jordan
and musical guest Lil Baby.

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:07.634
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

